update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('715bd9b1-bdba-4a58-b4c1-347624aa2904'::uuid,'e3e88d0c67d742f1033dc45a6d47e638','Circular reasoning means the premise already assumes the conclusion, so the argument proves nothing new. Here the premise is about the last two years and the conclusion is about this year. Those are different claims: the premise could be true and this year could still go the other way. The evidence is thin, but it is real evidence.'),
('801a0124-4d13-4853-97cf-dfd866f167cd'::uuid,'b988d795e2140ea0458c2836b0abd4a0','Translate the abstract words back into the argument. The phrase "an inference about a future event" is the prediction about this year''s rain. The phrase "a very limited number of instances of related past events" is the two previous years. That matches our prediction exactly: two years is too small a record to support even a "probably" about this year.

The move to repeat: when an argument predicts the future from the past, count the past cases. If there are only a handful, look for an answer about a small or limited sample, often worded abstractly like this one.'),
('c771334e-fe3c-4b6f-b518-b8f60f1d0252'::uuid,'0f762642be81852308b93d584b5884a3','The argument never mentions averages or one unusually wet year, so it can''t overemphasize that possibility. It doesn''t raise it at all. If anything, a critic would say the author ignores how one strange year could distort a two-year record. C describes the opposite of what the author does.'),
('34058bef-aa1d-4f1f-a642-9d0d1379bebc'::uuid,'6eabd2b5e5279df00ede803b42ac99bb','D describes concluding that two things are linked because they often show up together. The author doesn''t conclude that anything is associated; the conclusion is a prediction about this year. And D''s "many instances" gets the evidence backwards: the problem here is that there are only two.'),
('3bd64a5f-7b4f-4cbc-ad3a-dff01f92d8de'::uuid,'cc17b35b1236e928ea1221cd2dd1aefa','The argument cites no source at all. The rainfall in the previous two years is stated as a premise, and on Flaw questions we accept premises as true. Even with perfect rainfall records, two years would still be too few, so reliability isn''t the problem.'),
('a97b10c9-de1c-47e4-be9b-a95dfa7610e7'::uuid,'692a376ae27eb753ab6f8cd30a002c5a','This is the broken analogy we predicted. A logician who is illogical in discussions of logic is failing at the core of the job, which is incompetence. A physician with an unhealthy lifestyle can still treat people well. So the two cases aren''t alike in the way the argument needs.

Run the stem''s test. The phrase "fails to take into account" means ask whether the idea would weaken the argument. It does. If the logician''s failure is incompetence and the physician''s isn''t, the physician example can''t earn logicians the same pass.

The move to repeat: on an analogy argument, the right Flaw answer usually names the relevant difference between the two cases.'),
('8226c5b7-99ba-4b58-9bfd-aa8d2607dc55'::uuid,'2b0449c8f3ce9f42a1aabb093b400edb','B is about a physician whose health "deteriorates badly," not one with an unhealthy lifestyle, which is what the argument discusses. And at most B questions the physician half of the analogy, which is the half that works. It says nothing about whether a logician must be logical when discussing logic.'),
('0709acf4-1129-4409-b210-403e29a4b695'::uuid,'1427c7b9c995fd548d2139c50681c2e2','The argument is about what each profession needs to do, not about which one causes more harm. Incompetent physicians could be far more dangerous than illogical logicians, and the question of whether logicians must be logical about logic would be untouched.'),
('57813257-6e00-4afa-9bc9-5735ba55b978'::uuid,'87d36354d0cb0762fd98b40f009f03c6','How hard a standard is to meet doesn''t decide whether it is required. Something can be very difficult and still be necessary for doing a job well. D compares difficulty, while the argument is about necessity, so it misses the flaw.'),
('b35bd705-24d2-4c21-a16a-936a63b35560'::uuid,'3c14fa6c8daac05db1f1d212f02c4e15','E agrees with the author: it accepts that logicians don''t need to be logical to be competent at discussing logic. Calling it "highly desirable" doesn''t contradict the claim that it isn''t necessary. A Flaw answer has to hurt the argument, and an answer that concedes the author''s point can''t be what the author failed to account for.'),
('dc667a9b-ed68-44e6-bace-b383a5cf854d'::uuid,'43eae0b23335fcb563eecb1c5d4feda8','A accuses the student of a motive: bringing up the criteria to distract from the paper''s weaknesses. Nothing in the argument tells us why the student is complaining. The flaw is in how the student reads the rule, and that flaw would be there whatever the motive.'),
('a694fcf5-b88b-4605-bd29-74ca5e19a713'::uuid,'0687fca02f921c28630f1ed25b761a96','B describes drawing a value judgment from facts alone. But the student''s conclusion isn''t really a value judgment: whether the paper was graded by the professor''s stated criteria is a question of fact about what the rule says and what grade was given. The student''s facts are fine. The error is treating a requirement for an A as if it guaranteed an A.'),
('c6682ca3-987b-4577-91cf-6217ea53ce46'::uuid,'8d65afe2d5092d823f77c74874c42051','This is the reversal we predicted, in abstract words. The phrase "a condition that is among the requirements for a particular grade" is reliable statistical evidence, which the word "only" makes required for an A. The phrase "a condition that is enough to guarantee that grade" is how the student treats it: I had reliable evidence, so I deserved an A.

The move to repeat: when a Flaw stimulus has "only," "only if," "requires," or "must," diagram it, then check which side the author treats as the trigger. If the author uses the necessary side to prove the sufficient side, the answer will say the argument confuses what is required with what is enough. The test can word that many ways: "necessary" vs. "sufficient," "required" vs. "guarantee," or, as here, "among the requirements" vs. "enough."'),
('2002da55-9b52-4313-b63b-21c1276408f6'::uuid,'98c82448e898db6aeac175ebf362eee0','D says the argument rests on a biased report. But the key fact isn''t the student''s word alone: the professor acknowledges that the conclusions were supported by reliable statistical evidence. And a neutral observer making this exact argument would commit the same error, so bias isn''t the flaw.'),
('8ec51bb9-82db-43ab-a252-5ded8f9b8d41'::uuid,'fef931d8a19032e1e35e283e720742fd','The student''s conclusion is about the professor''s stated criteria, not about some objective standard of quality. The argument never brings up objective criteria, so it has no need to distinguish them. The flaw is a misreading of the professor''s own rule.'),
('b0fdbcb6-08fc-41a1-b078-dbc139e95525'::uuid,'14f0ded44764ea3f628d9df76d6e6ef3','Lindsey doesn''t rely on the critics'' opinions; Lindsey argues against them. Whether those unnamed people were well informed doesn''t matter to the reasoning, because their view is never used as evidence. A describes building a case on someone''s opinion, which is the reverse of what happens here.'),
('1d96afcb-3473-4255-8cd0-e5055612f01a'::uuid,'920837ed61568bda097d43a391a1df11','This matches the prediction. In B, "an opinion" is the claim that the company was unfair. The phrase "one potential reason for that opinion" is the bonus promise, which Lindsey only guesses the critics had in mind. And "has been undermined" is the point that profits fell, so the promise never required bonuses. From that alone, Lindsey infers the opinion is false and the company was fair.

The word "merely" carries the criticism: one failed reason isn''t enough to prove the opposite. The move to repeat: when an author attacks an opponent''s reason and then concludes the opponent''s claim is false, look for Unproven vs. Proven False. Showing that a reason fails leaves the claim unproven, not disproven.'),
('ba7fece3-e72c-48a5-b387-12b303ec07ce'::uuid,'450d0e0b195a89ce548883c6c9e1fa19','C describes an attack on the people: dismissing a claim because of who made it. Lindsey says nothing about the critics'' character, motives, or qualifications. Lindsey goes after a reason for their claim, the bonus promise, so the attack is on an argument, not on people.'),
('c9842652-47f5-414e-825b-5d27e75dcc93'::uuid,'c611975d116c5508c98435e37b8e2b4f','D is tempting because the argument is about profits. But there''s no confusion. The promise was about an increase over last year''s profits, and Lindsey compares this year''s profit to last year''s, which is exactly the right comparison. A profit much smaller than last year''s is not an increase. The math is fine; the error is the leap from one failed reason to the claim that the company acted fairly.'),
('fa1fdb14-7877-44a0-b4f2-08a3ab77768b'::uuid,'3e88ed640dfc9cf03be803b82332476e','Run the test: "overlooks the possibility" means ask whether the idea would weaken the argument. E says a policy can be fair without being generous. That helps Lindsey, who argues the company was fair even though it paid no bonuses. An idea that supports the conclusion can''t be what the author failed to consider.'),
('52c90357-4b43-4f66-93c1-ec9997421685'::uuid,'29baa751e3cb101a06f8f25794be44d0','Equivocation needs a key word to change meaning between premise and conclusion. Here "environment" means the same natural world throughout: energy production damages it, and making products damages it. The problem is that the columnist counts only one kind of damage, not that the word shifts.'),
('8fe20652-cc5e-49fd-b005-dd0180a87280'::uuid,'ee08495846bf3dbd0d5e0c989b20bf2d','B says the argument mixes up cause and effect. But the argument''s only causal claim runs the normal way: energy production causes environmental damage. The columnist never treats the damage as a cause of energy use, so nothing is reversed.'),
('791b7888-aab2-412d-9de0-a73002295b4c'::uuid,'79bc8aad0a0fda5f158cb18fedb3f0f2','C would fit if the columnist drew a conclusion about all recycled products from a few. But the conclusion is limited to "some types of products," the same products the premise is about ("those products"). The argument never generalizes, so representativeness isn''t an issue.'),
('86fb2385-42cc-469a-bbe2-a6555e8fe4da'::uuid,'4da067fdb7fb019c7c828be3b9c8a3db','This matches the prediction. The columnist compared the two options on one factor, energy, found a tie, and declared them tied overall. D names what got left out: making products from recycled materials "may have environmental benefits unrelated to energy consumption," such as less mining or logging, or less landfill waste.

Run the test for a "fails to consider" answer: it means ask whether the idea would weaken the argument. If recycling helps the environment in ways that have nothing to do with energy, then equal energy use doesn''t make the two options equally damaging. The conclusion falls.

The move to repeat: when an argument compares two options on one factor and draws a conclusion about the whole, look for the answer naming another factor that could tip the balance.'),
('a1c1f9e4-c8dd-42f5-81c3-a2eb7b5dc008'::uuid,'1825f2c03c0dd2caf24f18922e8422e6','E describes the post hoc flaw: one thing followed another, so the first caused the second. Nothing in the argument relies on timing or sequence. The claim that energy production damages the environment is stated as a general premise, and on Flaw questions we accept premises as true.'),
('f9f704d2-75c0-441d-b3df-1f5701a4ea93'::uuid,'f07eec6a29d7f44665cb1962af346965','A describes reversed causation: treating something that resulted from the drop as if it caused the drop. The fine increase came first, six months ago, and was made to help pay for the garage, not in response to fewer violations. Nothing here that came after the drop is being cast as its cause.'),
('71454257-6260-48fe-bcae-03452f3d21b4'::uuid,'d07f7eef8ad1238d688a276d7e868163','B is the most tempting wrong answer because it sounds like a real worry: maybe a second raise won''t work as well. But check what the official needs. The conclusion only says raising fines again would bring "even fewer" violations. Any further drop would do. Negate B: a second raise reduces violations less than the first did. The conclusion can still be true, so the argument doesn''t take B for granted.

Compare that with E. If the garage caused the drop, the fines may do nothing at all, and the recommendation collapses.'),
('e63d7d36-7191-4c25-ba4e-24c505ce84b5'::uuid,'209c203965e152462a036a40dab701fc','The city''s income from fines has nothing to do with whether higher fines reduce violations. The conclusion is about getting fewer violations, and money the city earns can''t weaken a claim about how fines change drivers'' behavior.'),
('97d5639c-5b3b-40a3-ba4a-769bb338a3f6'::uuid,'e09fb0b8c2626d441e8c2ac10ec2365a','The official doesn''t need illegal parkers to prefer parking legally. A higher fine can push people into legal spaces they dislike. Negate D: illegal parkers would rather keep parking illegally. Fines could still deter them, so the argument survives, which means D isn''t something it assumes.'),
('2e26a5ce-8279-402a-83d3-32a74fc07b18'::uuid,'8ca41df6084e969904363afdf92c3e37','This is the alternate cause we predicted. The garage opened at the same time the fines went up, so either change could explain the 50 percent drop. If the drop came from the new parking spaces, raising fines again may do nothing, and the recommendation falls apart.

E''s wording, "fails to establish," means the argument needed this and didn''t prove it. To recommend higher fines, the official had to rule out the garage, and didn''t.

The move to repeat: when an argument credits one change for a result, scan the stimulus for anything else that changed at the same time. The rival cause is often planted in a clause that reads like background, like "to help pay for the parking garage that had just opened."'),
('268e95ee-e05e-4d2a-b849-2743ae683754'::uuid,'ee0e47960857373a62095eacb32431d5','Circular reasoning means the premise already assumes the conclusion. Here the premise is a survey result and the conclusion is about why attendance is low. Those are different claims. The result was close to guaranteed by who was asked, which can feel circular, but that is a sampling problem, not a premise restating the conclusion.'),
('0e039f0a-d733-4a62-aae3-6886e4a2369b'::uuid,'093847652b399bbf81784a7efba1d1e6','This matches the prediction. In B, "a sample that is likely to be unrepresentative" is the members present at a Tuesday meeting. The phrase "making a generalization" is the conclusion about why the club has an attendance problem. The survey asked only the people who made it on a Tuesday whether Tuesday works for them, so the members with Tuesday conflicts were screened out by the way the survey was taken.

The move to repeat: whenever a conclusion rests on a survey, ask who got the survey and who didn''t. If the method leaves out the very people the conclusion is about, the answer is a sampling flaw.'),
('63e9d8a9-3290-40f6-9ca8-c819f97bd630'::uuid,'b9a357e3635deef788279cac06c66e47','C is tempting because 95 percent is not 100 percent. But the president didn''t treat the result as having no exceptions. The conclusion is that the problem was not due "primarily" to schedule conflicts, which allows for some conflicts. The flaw is who was surveyed, not how far the president stretched the number.'),
('7fcfd3b5-c828-4f69-9b0e-fbc8f572559d'::uuid,'85b0a79cda1228cad7f2833623c73e17','No two premises conflict. A survey was taken at a Tuesday meeting, and 95 percent of those surveyed had no difficulty attending on Tuesdays. Both can be true together. The premises are consistent; they just don''t support the conclusion.'),
('677719a9-bcb3-4dfb-930f-431cd67cedc7'::uuid,'ca0f0daf705cc2e81a60fcf573294152','E describes a necessary vs. sufficient error about a change that won''t solve a problem. The president never claims that moving the meeting wouldn''t be enough to fix attendance and then infers it isn''t needed. The argument runs from a survey to a conclusion about the cause, with no conditional reasoning at all.'),
('bffd12d7-ab84-4100-bc84-b6dac33c0d0c'::uuid,'fd8348fbbf574059cd371acbf9901b3a','Check the conclusion''s exact words: we would spend less on "these ozone-related health costs." If other pollutants rose, other health costs would rise, not ozone-related ones. So A attacks a claim about total health costs that the author never made.'),
('7b3adbf5-ce82-444a-89e9-6a0888d37b9d'::uuid,'0a77a8d9db0b444ca4bac32dff6447e7','This names the assumption we predicted. The author got from $5 billion to about $1 billion by taking one fifth, which only works if ozone-related health costs vary "roughly in proportion to ozone levels." The argument offers no evidence for that. If costs only kick in above some threshold, a one fifth cut in ozone could save far less than a fifth of the costs.

Run the test for a "presumes" answer: negate it and see if the argument survives. Health costs don''t vary in proportion to ozone? Then the billion-dollar figure has no support. So the argument depends on B.

The move to repeat: when an argument turns a percentage change in one thing into the same percentage change in another, look for a proportionality assumption.'),
('bc16125a-23d5-4830-b3a5-c92451a14cc0'::uuid,'92e3d8e66cfce5f46b0170f136d1e6e6','The conclusion is conditional: we would save the money "should the proposed measures be adopted." It never claims the measures will be adopted, so it owes us no reason to think they will be. C criticizes the argument for not proving something it never claimed.'),
('26867d8f-c1cd-426d-9948-f0ad3dc938da'::uuid,'62adc136461ff5c8eb8f21bc142baf04','The argument contains no appeal to emotion. It runs on numbers: a one fifth reduction, over $5 billion, about a billion. The flaw is in that math, not in an attempt to stir up feelings.'),
('71a7fb4c-55ec-4137-8f2a-6c4ecdb78188'::uuid,'be5c455e04ff3db0318ec3d3f94d559a','E accuses the author of a motive: using air pollution to distract from bigger health costs. Nothing in the argument suggests a hidden purpose. The topic is ozone-related costs, and the argument stays on it. The error is a bad assumption about those costs, not a change of subject.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;